[288,4,0,1863,"Bridge Across the Tennessee River","Union army railroad crews rebuilt bridges destroyed in the Civil War so quickly that Lincoln joked one was made of beanpoles and cornstalks. Photographs of the U.S. Military Railroad show how much engineering lay behind the war.","How would you build a bridge in a few days?"],
[290,0,0,1863,"Lewis Carroll","Lewis Carroll, whose real name was Charles Dodgson, wrote Alice's Adventures in Wonderland. He was also a keen photographer, and here the photographer Oscar Rejlander captured him in a relaxed pose.","What do you think inspired Alice's wonderland?"],
[294,0,0,1863,"Lincoln Before Gettysburg","Alexander Gardner photographed Abraham Lincoln on November 8, 1863, eleven days before the president gave the Gettysburg Address. The portrait was made into small cabinet cards that people bought and collected.","What do you notice about his tired, serious face?"],
[299,0,0,1864,"George Sand","George Sand was a French novelist who wrote under a man's name and wore trousers when most women did not. Nadar photographed her in a soft light, wrapped in a large cloak.","Why might a woman choose a man's name for her books?"],
[301,4,0,1864,"Rebel Works in Front of Atlanta","George Barnard followed General Sherman's army and photographed Atlanta's earth fortifications, with a cannon and a tent behind. His photographs were published in a large album of Sherman's campaign.","What do you think happened to the trees?"],
[302,0,0,1864,"Julia Jackson","Julia Jackson was Julia Margaret Cameron's niece and one of the most photographed beauties of her day. She later became the mother of the writer Virginia Woolf and the painter Vanessa Bell.","What do you think she is thinking about?"],
[305,0,0,1864,"Sarah Bernhardt","Sarah Bernhardt, then about twenty, became one of the greatest actresses in the world, known as the Divine Sarah. Nadar photographed her in a dark gown with her face turned in profile.","How does she hold her head?"],
[306,0,0,1864,"Sojourner Truth","Sojourner Truth escaped slavery and became a famous speaker for freedom and women's rights. She sold cards like this one to earn money, with the line \"I sell the shadow to support the substance.\"","What do you think she meant by shadow and substance?"],
[311,1,0,1865,"Section of the Grizzly Giant","The Grizzly Giant is an ancient sequoia tree in Yosemite's Mariposa Grove. Watkins had men stand next to it to show how enormous it was, and photographs like this helped win protection for the trees.","How many people stand next to the tree? How big does it make them look?"],
[312,1,0,1865,"First View of the Yosemite Valley","Watkins photographed the valley from the edge of a cliff, with El Capitan and Bridal Veil Fall across it. This became the classic view, and photographers have returned to it ever since.","Which cliff do you think is El Capitan?"],
[313,0,0,1865,"Alfred, Lord Tennyson","People nicknamed this portrait of the poet Tennyson \"the Dirty Monk\" because of his shaggy hair and dark cloak. Julia Margaret Cameron's dramatic close-ups made her sitters look like heroes from legends.","Does he look like a monk, a poet or a wizard?"],
[314,4,1,1865,"A Burial Party, Cold Harbor","John Reekie photographed soldiers collecting remains from the battlefield at Cold Harbor, Virginia, after the war. It is a hard picture to look at, but it made people think about the price of war.","Why do you think the photographer went back to a battlefield?"],
[316,4,0,1865,"Ruins of the Pinckney Mansion, Charleston","Charleston, South Carolina, where the Civil War began, was battered by fire and cannon fire. George Barnard photographed its ruined buildings in 1865, as the war ended.","What would you want to rebuild first?"],
[317,4,0,1865,"Ruins of the Railroad Depot, Charleston","Tall chimneys stand in the empty shell of a burned station. Barnard's photos show how much of the South lay in ruins.","How many chimneys can you count?"],
[319,2,0,1865,"Rue de la Montagne-Sainte-Geneviève","Charles Marville photographed this narrow old Paris street before it was changed for good. Wet cobblestones, shuttered shops and tall houses make it feel like another century.","Does this look like a street you would like to walk down?"],
[320,2,0,1865,"St. Peter's Basilica, Rome","Inside the world's largest church, a giant bronze canopy stands under a huge dome. James Anderson photographed Rome for visitors who could not travel there.","Look at the people. How big is the canopy compared with them?"],
[321,4,0,1865,"Sherman and His Generals","General William Tecumseh Sherman sits with his commanders at the end of the Civil War. George Barnard photographed Sherman's army, and this portrait shows the men who led it.","Which one do you think is Sherman?"],
[323,0,0,1865,"Édouard Manet","Édouard Manet was a French painter who helped begin modern art. Nadar, who was friends with many artists and writers, photographed him as a thoughtful young man.","What do you think he saw when he looked at his own paintings?"],
[324,1,0,1865,"Bridal Veil, Yosemite","Bridal Veil Fall drops about 620 feet down a cliff in Yosemite. Watkins made huge prints on glass plates the size of a small table, so people in cities felt as if they stood in the valley.","What sound do you think the waterfall makes?"],
[330,1,0,1866,"The Spanish Peaks","William Henry Jackson photographed two great peaks in southern Colorado. Before cameras, most Americans could only imagine what the West looked like.","What do you think the weather is like up there?"],
[331,0,0,1866,"Queen Victoria","Queen Victoria loved photography. She and Prince Albert collected thousands of photographs. André Disdéri, the cartes-de-visite king, photographed her in a dark dress.","Why do you think she liked collecting photographs?"],
